<p>China has opened its annual “two sessions” in Beijing, bringing together the National People’s Congress (NPC) and the Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference (CPPCC). The meetings are where leaders signal policy priorities, review budgets and personnel changes, and lay out the year’s economic goals. They’re closely watched at home and abroad because they offer one of the clearest, regularly scheduled windows into Beijing’s plans for the world’s second-largest economy. This year, the agenda is expected to spotlight China’s push into high-tech areas such as artificial intelligence, even as growth cools and the property slump continues to weigh on the economy, according to previews of the meetings. Observers are also looking for clues on defense spending and the next five-year plan, along with more detail on real estate restructuring and on healthcare reimbursement and drug pricing.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DUexvAI3DRjTOaAW98eVFCQcygT0nnBR266PPzDOf6M8Mocl-12ie6fvX6sB8ROeyn2r3iZkUtaz0ZvKgwXflXzTSKJERZ9E5P6MGjxyk2ETNOChN_BJtmQ8w9UvjMR8qAoA1Yec_z-YZxdK-JxZCfbhvudO7oDHPUheK1KsKBWoU83khsz5t1ceZtRhnk1xF4ZO6ILuz7XK9-bXS2ztiwp-HA' alt='A view near Beijing’s Great Hall of the People ahead of China’s annual parliamentary meetings.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What matters most: The premier’s work report, the headline economic targets, and the state budget are the main outputs that officials and markets use to gauge policy direction.</li><li>Scale and timeline: Thousands of delegates travel to Beijing for a roughly two-week run of scripted speeches, closed-door discussions, and formal votes.</li><li>A tech push, under pressure: Previews cast AI and other high-tech goals against soft demand and broader growth headwinds—an ambitious agenda running up against real constraints.</li><li>Looking for real property fixes: Investors are watching for more than broad promises of support—especially any concrete steps to restructure the sector and reset expectations for housing and developer financing.</li><li>Defense signals beyond the topline: The defense budget number matters, but so does the language leaders use to frame security priorities amid scrutiny of the military establishment ahead of the meetings.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chinese leadership agenda (as described by PBS): Preview coverage frames the meetings as a test of how Xi Jinping’s high-tech, AI-driven ambitions fit with slower growth—pushing innovation while confronting the economy’s limits. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/chinas-economic-ambitions-hit-limits-to-growth-as-its-national-congress-prepares-to-meet'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Policy-watchers on global signals (Firstpost): Firstpost’s explainer says outside attention will land on the growth target, defense spending, and the new five-year plan as clues to Beijing’s domestic resilience and external posture. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/china-two-sessions-2026-defence-budget-five-year-plan-explained-13985898.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li><li>Market participants (SCMP): SCMP reports that investors are scanning the agenda for practical guidance on property restructuring and on drug pricing and reimbursement rules that could meaningfully affect businesses. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/business/article/3345261/investors-watch-chinas-two-sessions-clues-property-overhaul'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Civic-legislative framing (ABC News/AP wire): ABC’s wire report describes the NPC as a largely ceremonial parliament, with leaders using the event to set policy and economic goals. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/china-unveil-priorities-coming-5-years-major-annual-130734358'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Process and political context (The Guardian/BBC): The Guardian and BBC stress the size and choreography of the gatherings, portraying them as the annual moment when legislation, budgets, and personnel changes are formally ratified. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c2lrxyke7kro'>BBC News</a>, <a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/04/china-two-sessions-2026-meeting-what-are-they-when-is-it-explainer'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/china-unveil-priorities-coming-5-years-major-annual-130734358'>China to unveil priorities for coming 5 years at major annual political meeting</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/chinas-economic-ambitions-hit-limits-to-growth-as-its-national-congress-prepares-to-meet'>China&#x27;s economic ambitions hit limits to growth as its national congress prepares to meet</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c2lrxyke7kro'>What to know about the &#x27;two sessions&#x27;,  China&#x27;s biggest political meeting</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/china-two-sessions-2026-defence-budget-five-year-plan-explained-13985898.html'>China’s Two Sessions 2026: What signals will Beijing send to the world?</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/04/china-two-sessions-2026-meeting-what-are-they-when-is-it-explainer'>China’s Two Sessions: what are the meetings and why do they matter?</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/business/china-business/article/3345310/healthcare-investors-keeping-eye-two-sessions-clarity-drug-pricing'>Healthcare investors keeping an eye on ‘two sessions’ for clarity on drug pricing</a> - scmp.com</li></ul>